        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. The picks were made from manufacturer product pages and specification tables,
            warranty terms and independent review coverage, all checked on 15 October 2026. Our
            <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon 7.0 AT review</a>
            is a specification review too, not a hands-on test.
          </p>
          <p>
            Five rules decided the list. <strong>On sale now:</strong> each machine had to be
            available to order on its maker's own store on the day we checked, under the name we
            use. <strong>Interval-friendly controls:</strong> dials, rollers or one-touch keys that
            change speed and incline without a long press, or, in the curved Sole's case, no buttons
            to press at all. <strong>A running deck:</strong> 60 inches long or more,
            so a full sprinting stride has room. <strong>Agreement:</strong> deck size, speed range,
            incline range and weight capacity had to match between the maker and at least one
            independent source. <strong>No safety action:</strong> we checked the US Consumer
            Product Safety Commission's recall database for every brand considered.
          </p>
          <p>
            Several well-known machines are missing on purpose. Matrix's retail T30, T50 and T75
            treadmills are named in a recall the CPSC announced in January 2026 over a power cord
            that can work loose and overheat, so we left Matrix out. The <a href="/peloton-tread/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Peloton Tread</a> was the
            subject of a CPSC recall in 2021 over a touchscreen that could detach; we did not
            re-verify its current specification for this page and have not ranked it. Horizon's
            T101 was recalled in 2022 because it could change speed or stop on its own — a serious
            problem on any treadmill and worse during a sprint — and it is not on this list either,
            although none of the Horizon models we do list is covered by that recall. The
            AssaultRunner Pro, the best-known curved sprint trainer, showed as sold out on
            Assault's own store, which says outstanding orders are being upgraded to new models,
            so we could not call it currently sold.
          </p>
          <p>
            We also left out SOLE, whose quick-select keys are among the best for intervals. SOLE
            redesigned the F80 and F85 for 2026 with different motors, decks and capacities while
            keeping the names, and most independent coverage still describes the earlier machines.
            Until independent sources catch up we could not cross-check enough figures to put
            either on a card here. Our reviews of the
            <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SOLE F80</a> and
            <a href="/sole-f85-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">SOLE F85</a>
            explain which figures conflict.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="speed-changes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How Fast Speed and Incline Change</h2>
<p>
            This is the specification that matters most for HIIT, and it is the one manufacturers
            almost never publish. No treadmill maker we checked gives an acceleration time from a
            walk to top speed. What you can compare is how the controls are laid out, and what
            independent reviewers report when they time the belt.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Three kinds of control</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Arrow buttons</strong> change speed in small steps, typically a tenth of a mile
            per hour per press, and they are the slowest way to start a sprint. Holding the button
            speeds it up, but you are still waiting several seconds before the belt even begins to
            accelerate in earnest. Arrow-only machines are fine for steady runs and frustrating for
            30-second efforts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>One-touch or quick-select keys</strong> jump straight to a preset speed or
            incline: press 10 and the machine heads for 10 mph. They are the classic HIIT control,
            and their weakness is that the presets are fixed. If your sprint pace is 9.4 mph and the
            keys are at 9 and 10, you press one and then adjust. Horizon's custom interval keys
            and NordicTrack's on-screen quick-adjust buttons both work on this principle.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Dials and rollers</strong>, such as Horizon's QuickDial, let you sweep the speed
            up or down with a thumb without letting go of the handlebar. Horizon says its rollers
            simplify speed and incline changes without breaking stride, and BarBend's review
            describes the rollers as more ergonomic than repeatedly pressing a button. For
            intervals at odd paces they are the most flexible control we found on a home treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the controls cannot change</h3>
          <p>
            However quick the control, the belt still has to accelerate. Every motorised treadmill
            ramps speed gradually, for good reason: an instant jump from 4 to 11 mph would throw
            you off the back. In practice that means the first few seconds of each sprint are spent
            catching up. One independent reviewer who timed several machines found the
            NordicTrack Commercial 2450 reached 12 mph several seconds sooner than the Horizon it
            was compared with, and described Horizon's 7.8 AT, the 7.4 AT's bigger sibling with the same controls, as quick to adjust as well. Neither
            gets there instantly, and nothing that runs on a motor does.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline is slower still. Raising a deck from flat to its maximum takes far longer than
            changing speed, and on a machine like Sunny's Versatile Treadmill, which climbs to 45%, a full sweep is a
            long wait. For incline intervals, change the grade a little before the work period
            starts, or build the session around fewer, longer climbs.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Working with the ramp</h3>
          <p>
            Two habits make any treadmill better for intervals. Start the speed change three or
            four seconds before the work period, so the belt arrives as the interval begins rather
            than halfway through it. And count your work interval from the moment the belt reaches
            speed, not from the button press. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-interval-workouts/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill interval workouts</a>
            are written with that lag in mind. If you would rather not touch the controls at all,
            a curved manual treadmill such as the Sole C80, or the RunFree mode on Wahoo's direct-sold
            KICKR RUN, takes the button out of the loop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="top-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. How Much Top Speed You Actually Need</h2>
<p>
            Most home treadmills top out at 10 or 12 mph. Every machine on this page reaches 12
            mph, one reaches 14 and one claims a 4:00-per-mile pace, which is 15 mph. Whether you
            need more than 12 depends on how fast you really run, not on how fast you would like
            the console to say you run. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-fast-do-treadmills-go/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how fast treadmills go</a>
            covers the ranges across the market.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Belt speed</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Pace per mile</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Who reaches it in intervals</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">8 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7:30</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Many recreational runners in short repeats</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">6:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fit club runners in hard intervals</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fast runners in short sprints</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">14 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4:17</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Competitive runners and strong sprinters</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">15 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4:00</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Very fast athletes in brief efforts</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The paces are simple arithmetic, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            has the full conversion. The honest conclusion is that a 12 mph treadmill is enough for
            the large majority of people doing HIIT. A 30-second effort at 10 or 11 mph is a hard
            sprint for most adults, and if you want more intensity without more speed, incline
            does the job: a sprint at 8 mph on a 6% grade is a brutal interval with a shorter, less
            punishing stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            You need more than 12 mph if you have hit the ceiling in real sessions, not just
            imagined doing so. Sprinters, fast 5K runners and athletes doing short maximal efforts
            are the people who press 12 and still have something left. For them, the Commercial
            2450's 14 mph is worth the extra money, and a curved trainer such as the C80 removes the
            ceiling problem in a different way, by letting the belt follow you.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more caution: treadmill speed displays are not always accurate, and a machine that
            reads 12 may be running slower or faster. Our page on
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            explains how to check before you build your training around the number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="motor-deck-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Motor, Deck and Frame: What Hard Use Requires</h2>
<p>
            Intervals are hard on a treadmill in a particular way. Each sprint asks the motor to
            accelerate the belt under load and then hold speed while your foot strikes slow it,
            hundreds of times a session. Each landing at speed loads the deck and frame harder than
            a jog does. These are the specifications that tell you whether a machine is built for
            that.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Motor rating</h3>
          <p>
            Look for a continuous rating, usually written CHP, rather than a peak figure. NordicTrack
            rates the Commercial 2450 at 4.25 CHP, and independent reviews agree. Horizon
            does not publish a horsepower figure for the 7.0 AT or 7.4 AT; reviewers quote one, but
            without the maker's own figure we have left it off the cards. What Horizon does publish
            is a lifetime motor warranty, which tells you more about how long the motor is expected
            to last than any horsepower number. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why a continuous rating and a long motor warranty matter more than the size
            of the number, and why peak ratings on budget machines mislead.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deck length and width</h3>
          <p>
            Sprinting lengthens your stride more than any other running, and a short deck forces
            you to shorten it or risk clipping the motor hood. Sixty inches is the standard for a
            running treadmill and the minimum we would accept for HIIT; every motorised machine here
            has a 60-inch deck, and the curved Sole C80's slat belt is 62 by 19 inches. Width
            matters too: 22 inches gives your arms and any lateral drift more room at speed than
            20. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            sets out the lengths for walking, running and sprinting.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Frame stability and capacity</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill that shakes at speed is unpleasant and, at sprint pace, unnerving. Two
            proxies help. Weight capacity is the first: a 375 or 400 lb rating usually comes with a
            heavier frame than a 300 lb one, even for a lighter runner, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-weight-capacity-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">weight capacity guide</a>
            explains why a margin above your own weight is wise. The second is whether the machine
            folds. Non-folding frames have fewer joints to flex; folding frames
            can be very stable, but they rely on a hinge and a lock. Garage Gym Reviews' testers
            report that the Horizon 7.0 AT did not shake during runs and sprints, which is the kind
            of reporting worth looking for in any review you read. If a treadmill you own does
            wobble, our guide to a
            <a href="/treadmill-shaking-or-wobbling/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">shaking or wobbling treadmill</a>
            covers the usual causes.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cushioning</h3>
          <p>
            All four motorised machines have cushioned decks; the curved C80 runs on rubber-topped
            slats instead. Softer is not automatically better for
            sprinting: a deck that gives a lot can feel slow underfoot when you push off hard. If
            you mainly do short, fast intervals, a firmer feel may suit you better than a soft
            one, and it is worth reading what reviewers say about the deck rather than relying on
            the marketing name of the cushioning system.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="curved-manual" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Curved Manual Treadmills for Sprints</h2>
<p>
            A curved manual treadmill has no motor at all. You drive the slatted belt with your own
            legs, and the curve means that moving forward on the deck speeds the belt up and
            drifting back slows it down. For sprinting that is a genuine advantage: the machine
            accelerates exactly as fast as you do, there is no button to press and no ceiling on
            speed. They are a staple of gyms that do a lot of conditioning work, and our
            <a href="/curved-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill guide</a>
            explains how they work and how they feel different from a motorised belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            When this page was first written, none was on the list, for reasons of supply rather
            than quality: on 15 October 2026 the AssaultRunner Pro and its siblings showed as sold out
            on Assault's own store, and we had not yet found an independent source to confirm the Sole
            C80's figures. Our later Sole guide did, matching Sole's listing against a retailer listing
            and independent coverage, so the C80 is now carded above. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best curved treadmills</a>
            covers the category in full, with current stock notes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before buying one for HIIT, be clear about the trade-offs. A curved manual treadmill is
            much harder work than a motorised one at the same speed, so it suits short efforts
            better than long intervals. It is poor for recovery jogs and slow walking, which many
            interval sessions need between efforts. It is expensive — the credible models cost about
            as much as the dearest motorised machines here. And it cannot hold you at an
            exact pace, which matters if your sessions are written as specific speeds. Our
            comparison of
            <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual and motorised treadmills</a>
            sets the two side by side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Safety Key, Mounting and Set-Up for Sprints</h2>
<p>
            Interval training is where treadmill accidents happen, because it involves the fastest
            speeds, the most fatigue and the most getting on and off a moving belt. A few habits
            matter more than any feature. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-tips/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill safety tips</a>
            cover the general rules; these are the ones specific to HIIT.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clip the safety key to your clothing</h3>
          <p>
            Every session, not just the hard ones. The magnetic key stops the belt if you drift too
            far back, and drifting back is exactly what happens in the last seconds of a sprint.
            Clip it at the waist where the cord will pull before you reach the end of the deck. If
            the key does not stop the belt when pulled, stop using the machine until it is fixed;
            our page on a <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safety key that is not working</a>
            explains the checks.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Mounting a moving belt</h3>
          <p>
            Some sprint protocols have you straddle the belt on the side rails during recovery and
            jump back on at speed. It saves the acceleration lag, and it is the single riskiest
            thing you can do on a treadmill. If you do it, hold the handrails firmly, step on with
            one foot to match the belt speed before committing your weight, and never do it at the
            very end of a session when your legs are spent. The safer alternative is to drop to a
            walk during recovery and let the belt ramp up, accepting a few seconds of lag. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-sprint-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill sprint workout</a>
            is written both ways.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Space and power</h3>
          <p>
            Leave clear space behind the treadmill: a fall at 10 mph carries you a long way, and a
            wall or a piece of furniture close behind the deck turns a stumble into an injury. Each
            manufacturer's manual gives a minimum clearance, and Wahoo, for example, specifies
            clearance behind and beside its KICKR RUN. Power matters more for sprinting machines
            than for walking ones. Horizon's specification table says its AT treadmills are not
            compatible with GFCI outlets, and Wahoo says its KICKR RUN needs a dedicated circuit
            and a non-GFCI outlet, noting that its fastest modes demand significant power. A hard
            acceleration that trips the breaker mid-sprint is both annoying and a fall risk; see
            our guides to a <a href="/treadmill-trips-breaker/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that trips the breaker</a>
            and <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">surge protectors</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stop for these</h3>
          <p>
            Stop using the treadmill and check it, or call the manufacturer, if the speed surges or
            drops on its own, the belt does not stop when the safety key is pulled, you smell
            burning, or the breaker trips each time you accelerate. The manual is the authority on
            your model.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            These are serious running treadmills, and several kinds of buyer will be better served
            elsewhere.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your budget is under about $1,000,</strong> do not stretch to a cheap machine
            sold on its top speed. Short decks and slow controls make intervals worse, not just
            less comfortable. Look for a 60-inch deck and quick-select keys first, accept 10 or 12
            mph, and read our guides to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-800/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $800</a>
            and <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best budget treadmills</a>.
            Or do your intervals on a hill or a track and save the money.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If your HIIT is mostly walking intervals</strong> — fast walks alternating with
            steep ones — you do not need 12 mph or a 22-inch deck. A good incline treadmill does the
            job; see the <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a>.
            A walking pad does not: its speed ceiling and controls are not built for intervals.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you have joint problems,</strong> all-out sprints may not be the right
            interval at all, and incline intervals at walking or jogging speed are sometimes suggested
            as a lower-impact alternative. That is a conversation for your doctor or a
            physiotherapist, not a treadmill review; our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-bad-knees/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills for bad knees</a>
            covers the equipment side.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you run long more than you run fast,</strong> the priorities shift towards
            motor cooling, cushioning and decline, and the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-marathon-training/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for marathon training</a>
            is the better starting point.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best treadmill for HIIT?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, a treadmill with quick speed and incline controls, a 60-inch deck and a 12 mph top speed. Of the machines we could verify, the Horizon 7.4 AT fits best, with QuickDial controls, a Sprint 8 program and a 22 by 60-inch deck. The Horizon 7.0 AT has the same controls for less. Faster sprinters should look at the NordicTrack Commercial 2450's 14 mph.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What top speed do you need on a treadmill for sprinting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, 12 mph is enough. That is a five-minute-mile pace, and a 30-second effort at 10 or 11 mph is already a hard sprint for most adults. You need more only if you regularly reach 12 mph in real sessions. If you want harder intervals without more speed, add incline instead: it raises the effort with a shorter stride and less impact.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are one-touch speed buttons worth it for interval training?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, or dials that do the same job. Arrow buttons change speed in small steps, so you spend part of each short interval waiting. One-touch keys jump to a preset speed and dials let you sweep to any speed with a thumb. Neither makes the belt accelerate instantly, so start the change a few seconds before the work period whichever control you have.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a curved manual treadmill better for sprinting?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For short, all-out sprints it can be, because the belt accelerates exactly as fast as you do and there is no speed limit or button lag. It is worse for recovery jogs, exact paces and long intervals, and it is more tiring at any given speed. The Sole C80 is the curved trainer on this page, with eight magnetic resistance levels and a 62 by 19-inch belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What motor size do you need for HIIT on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a continuous rating, written CHP, and a long motor warranty rather than a peak horsepower figure. Running treadmills built for hard use are typically rated somewhere around 3 to 4 CHP or more, and the NordicTrack machines here are rated at 4.25 CHP. Some makers, including Horizon, publish a lifetime motor warranty instead of a rating, which is arguably the more useful promise.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to jump on and off a moving treadmill during sprints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is the riskiest thing you can do on a treadmill. If you straddle the belt for recovery, hold the rails firmly and step on with one foot first to match the belt speed before committing your weight. The safer option is to slow to a walk between efforts and accept the few seconds the belt takes to speed up. Always clip the safety key to your clothing.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for HIIT</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these comes from buying for the number on the box rather than for what an interval session actually asks of the machine.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for top speed alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 12 mph sticker on a machine with a short deck and arrow-only controls makes a worse interval treadmill than a 10 mph machine with quick keys and a 60-inch deck. Speed you cannot reach quickly or run safely is not useful. Check the controls and the deck length first, then the top speed.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing peak horsepower</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Budget machines often quote a peak rating that is two or three times what the motor sustains. Interval training is exactly the use that exposes the difference, because each sprint loads the motor hard. Compare continuous ratings where they are published, and the length of the motor warranty where they are not.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the electrical requirements</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Fast treadmills draw a lot of current when they accelerate under load. Several manufacturers specify a dedicated circuit or a non-GFCI outlet, and plugging into a shared circuit or a cheap extension lead is how a sprint ends with a tripped breaker. Read the installation section of the manual before you choose where the treadmill goes.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the safety key</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">People who always clip the key on a steady run often leave it dangling during intervals, because it feels like it gets in the way. Intervals are when you are most likely to drift back off the deck, so they are when the key matters most. Clip it to your waistband before the first effort, every session.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

